EXT. SPLIT CEDAR RAFT - FLOODED VALLEY - DAY

A river has swallowed a valley. Rooftops poke from the brown water like islands. A raft of lashed cedar logs turns in a slow eddy.

LEONARD "LEN" HARGROVE (60s, retired schoolteacher, ripped coat) lies on his back. EMEKA ABARA (30s, lean, shirtless, bleeding from the scalp) kneels and paddles with a broken oar.

LEN
Emeka. You paddle like a man drowning.

EMEKA
I am drowning, Len.

LEN
No. You're sitting down. There's a difference.

He pushes himself upright, winces, and takes the oar. Emeka lets him. He watches the roofs drift by.

LEN (CONT'D)
That one's the Bellamy place. I taught their boy.

EMEKA
He was in your class?

LEN
Sixth grade. He ate glue.

Emeka laughs. It turns into coughing. He spits red. Len puts the oar down.

EMEKA
I'm fine.

LEN
You're not fine. You're bleeding.

EMEKA
I got hit by a log. He knocked me out earlier. I'll live.

LEN
Don't say that. You'll jinx it.

He tears a strip off his coat and presses it to Emeka's scalp. Emeka flinches, then lets him. For a moment, neither speaks.

A shape in the water: an upturned rowboat, half-sunk, drifting toward them. Len points. Emeka grabs the oar and paddles hard. He reaches the hull, grabs it, and hauls it against the raft. He peers underneath. A sealed barrel is lashed to the thwart.

EMEKA (CONT'D)
Len, there's a barrel!

LEN
Don't open it. It'll sink us.

EMEKA
Help me!

Len hesitates. Then he drops to his knees and heaves with him. He pops the lid. Dry bread, a tin of matches, a jug of water. Emeka grabs the jug and drinks. Len stares at the matches, then at Emeka, then at the boat, which drifts away and slowly disappears.

LEN (CONT'D)
(softly)
He's been looking after us all along.

EMEKA
Who?

LEN
The Bellamy boy. It's his boat.

Len laughs, a thin, cracked sound. Emeka passes him the jug.
